tage()` divides by `10.0` when it should divide by `100.0`:\n\n```python\ndef percentage(string, match):\n    return float(string[:-1]) / 10.0   # should be / 100.0\n```\n\nSo `parse(\"{:%}\", \"50%\")` gives `5.0` instead of `0.5`, and `\"100%\"` gives `10.0` instead of `1.0`. The failing tests are `test_percentage_type` (5 of its 6 inputs fail; `0%` passes by coincidence), `test_percentage_function_directly` and `test_percentage_with_following_field`. Changing `10.0` to `100.0` should make all 140 pass, but I haven't tried that because you asked me not to touch the library. I left these tests failing on purpose; I can mark them as expected failures if you'd rather have a green run.\n\n**What the tests cover:**\n- **Field types:** plain, positional, named, dotted, hyphenated and nested (`a[b][c]`) fields, and repeated field names.\n- **Number types:** every number format (`d n b o x X f F e E g G %`), including signs, `0x`/`0b`/`0o` prefixes, digit grouping (`,` and `_`), `.0f`, nan/inf and `Decimal`.\n- **Text types:** `w W s S D l`.\n- **Format specs:** width, precision, alignment and fill characters (including regex special characters), plus `extract_format`.\n- **Dates and times:** all the built-in date/time formats (`ti tg ta te th tc tt ts`, including time zones and AM/PM at 12 o'clock) and strftime-style formats like `%Y-%m-%d`.\n- **The rest of the API:** `int_convert`, `FixedTzOffset`, custom types made with `with_pattern`, `search`/`findall` with start and end positions, `evaluate_result=False`, case sensitivity, positions of matches in the string, pickling, and `Parser` properties.\n\nFive of my first expectations were wrong and I corrected them to match what the library actually does:\n- When a width is given, the result keeps its padding: `{:>5}` on `\"   ab\"` returns `\"   ab\"`. Padding is only stripped when there's an alignment and no width.\n- `fixed_fields` reports regex group numbers, not positions.\n- `findall`'s fields match as little text as possible, so `>{}<` returns `\"some \"`, not `\"some <b\"`.\n- In `{:^<}`, the library reads `^` as the alignment, not the fill character, so `^` can't be used as fill. That's a limitation, and I left it out of the fill-character test.","ttft_ms":1875,"type":"result","duration_ms":150431,"uuid":"217fd9b9-99f6-49c1-9b22-dc0bb0328c42","ttft_stream_ms":988,"time_to_request_ms":203,"first_content_frame_ms":988,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
